This indicator is collected through the activities of the PPI and the PKI, support to the (6) six oil palm cooperatives, support to INTERMANGUE, support to the OIPI for the creation of a collective mark and a geographical indication. 
A template for a sheet to document innovations has been developed and made available to stakeholders.
Discussions with stakeholders within the framework of the PPI have identified the following innovations: 
1.	mechanization in the maintenance of plots;
2.	raising awareness on gender-based violence;
3.	digital training tool on financial inclusion;
4.	beekeeping in the cultivation of oil palm;
5.	Digitization of a training course on good agricultural practices.
6.	National certification of oil palm. 
7.	Mobile geolocation application.
Within the framework of the PKI, the two innovations identified to date are:
1.	a laboratory specialising in in vitro micro-propagation;
2.	a laboratory specializing in the quality control of shea butter and by-products.

The final target value for this indicator has been reached.
This indicator was collected through the activities listed below: 
16,743 actors, including 6,907 women beneficiaries of training, distributed as follows: 
1.	1,593 learners in the PPI, including 364 women; 
2.	9,083 cooperative producers trained, including 1,058 women through the FFS;
3.	31 PPI agricultural supervisors, including 11 women;
4.	2 supervisors as part of the PPI;
5.	10 cluster specialists, including 2 women from the PPI;
6.	25 trainers trained and coached, including 13 women, in the methodology of training in personal initiative, entrepreneurial training based on psychology;
7.	571 women trained as part of the personal initiative training, entrepreneurial training based on psychology, in the mango and shea sectors;
8.	3,288 women trained as part of the capacity building of 3,000 women in the shea sector on behalf of the Shea Ivory Programme (PKI);
9.	3,067 young people trained, including 1,591 women.

Training provided to the farmers or workers, including women, benefiting from the project. In particular farmers and workers in the supported value chains, receiving training on: post-harvest handling of agricultural products, use of digitial technology, methods and skills related to testing, piloting, prototyping products; personal initiative, etc.

347 actors, including 43 women, benefiting from training, distributed as follows:&nbsp;
- 307 learners in the PPI, including 34 women;
- 20 producers fro m the COOPALEN Coop C.A. cooperative;
- 12 PPI agricultural supervisors, including 6 women;
- 08 engineers, including 3 women.

